<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandita Ramabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pandita Ramabai Sarasvati, Ramabai Dongre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pandita Ramabai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a> was an Indian social reformer and Christian missionary, born Ramabai Dongre into a Marathi-speaking Chitpavan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> family. Taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> by her father, she was the first woman to receive the titles Pandita and Sarasvati from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, in 1878. Widowed after an inter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> marriage considered improper at the time, she founded the Arya Mahila Samaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> to promote women's education and oppose child marriage, and gave evidence before the Hunter Commission. She converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, raised funds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and founded the Sharada Sadan for child widows in 1889 and the Mukti Mission at Kedgaon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandita-ramabai.jpg" alt="Pandita Ramabai" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramabai Sarasvati, Pandita, 1858-1922 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pandita_Ramabai_Sarasvati_1858-1922_front-page-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramabai Dongre was born on 23 April 1858 into a Marathi-speaking Chitpavan Brahmin family. Her father, Anant Shastri Dongre, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholar, taught her Sanskrit at home, and he had also taught her mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, against the prevailing custom. The family earned a meagre living by reciting the Purana at pilgrimage sites across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Orphaned at sixteen during the Great Famine of 1876 to 1878, Ramabai and her brother Srinivas continued to travel and recite Sanskrit scriptures. Her fame reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, where her address in the senate hall of the university was well received, and in 1878 the University of Calcutta conferred on her the titles Pandita and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a>.</p><p>After Srinivas died in 1880 she married Bipin Behari Medhvi, a Bengali lawyer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayastha</a> community, in a civil ceremony on 13 November 1880. The marriage was inter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and inter-regional and thought improper at the time. Their daughter Manorama was born in 1881, and Medhvi died of cholera on 4 February 1882.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ramabai moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and founded the Arya Mahila Samaj to promote women's education and deliverance from child marriage. She gave evidence to the Hunter Commission on education, urging that teachers be trained, women school inspectors appointed and Indian women admitted to medical colleges. In 1883 she went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where she was refused a place in medical training because of progressive deafness, and there she converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, citing her disillusionment with what she saw as orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>'s low regard for women. She kept some independence from her Anglican mentors, retaining her vegetarian diet and rejecting the doctrine of the Trinity.</p><p>From 1886 she spent two years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, lecturing and publishing The High-Caste Hindu Woman, and raised the equivalent of 60,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> for a school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. She returned in 1889 and founded Sharada Sadan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> for child widows. When several students converted she lost the backing of Pune's Hindu reformers, and she moved the school to Kedgaon as the Mukti Mission. During the famine of 1896 she toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>'s villages in bullock carts and brought thousands of outcast children, widows and orphans into its shelter. By 1900 it had 1,500 residents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramabai translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> from the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and Greek. She met opposition from conservative upper-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> elites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">B. G. Tilak</a>, who attacked her work in his paper Kesari. She received the Kaisar-i-Hind Medal in 1919. Her daughter Manorama died in 1921, and Ramabai died of septic bronchitis on 5 April 1922, aged 63. Scholars of Pentecostalism have pointed to a revival at the Mukti Mission in June 1905 as part of the global revivals from which the movement arose. The mission still operates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandita Ramabai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1857 the British avoided intervening in religion, even over the remarriage of Hindu child widows, though sati had been banned before the rebellion; Indian reformers took up the cause, from <b>Pandita Ramabai</b>, who championed the remarriage of Brahmin widows, to Gokhale’s Servants of India Society.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangaluru</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Kedgaon, Pune district, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.87,74.84&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Mangaluru on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6604 1684 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pandita Ramabai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7054 1954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7054 1954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.87,74.84&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In ninety-nine cases out of a hundred, the educated men of this country are opposed to Female Education and the proper position of woman.&#8221;</p><footer>Evidence to the Education Commission, 1882 (The High-Caste Hindu Woman, 1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;After many years of careful observation and thought, I have come to the conclusion that the chief needs of high-caste Hindu women are:– 1st, Self-Reliance; 2nd, Education; 3rd, Native Women Teachers.&#8221;</p><footer>The High-Caste Hindu Woman (1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…who have done their best to keep women in a state of complete dependence and ignorance, vehemently deny that this has anything to do with the present degradation of the Hindu nation.&#8221;</p><footer>The High-Caste Hindu Woman (1887) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/ramabai/woman/woman.html" rel="noopener">digital.library.upenn.edu</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
